<Page title="How we check facts: editorial and sourcing policy" description="The rules every FBA Tactics page follows: primary sources only, a claims register, a separate checking pass, a named editor, dated checks and public corrections." kicker="Standards" photo="hub-about" updated="November 11, 2026" lede="If you find a page that breaks one of these rules, tell us and we will fix it in public.">
  <h2>1. Only the issuing source</h2><p>A fact is published only if it comes from the body that issues it: a regulation in the eCFR, a Federal Register notice, a public law, an IRS publication, a state revenue department, or a marketplace's own public fee or policy page. Third-party blogs, agencies, forums, news summaries and wikis are not cited even when they are right; a marketplace's own seller blog or announcements page counts as that marketplace's publication. Software vendors are cited only in the <a href="/reviews/">seller software</a> section, and only as the source for what they say about their own product and price — never for rules, fees or facts about anyone else. If a reader cannot open the source without logging in, we do not rely on it.</p>
  <h2>2. Every fact is registered</h2><p>Each factual statement links to an entry in a claims register that records the claim, the source address, the publisher, the date it was opened, a short excerpt proving it, who drafted it and who checked it. The site's build refuses to publish a page that cites an unchecked entry.</p>
  <h2>3. A separate pass checks every fact</h2><p>Drafting and checking are both done with AI tools, as separate passes: the checking pass reopens every source and is recorded under a different identity in the claims register, and a page cannot be built while any of its facts is unchecked. It is an automated check, not a human fact-checker. The checker reopens each source and confirms numbers, dates, units and conditions; anything that fails is corrected from the source or removed.</p>
  <h2>4. A named editor is responsible</h2><p>Every page is published under the responsibility of the site's editor, <a href="/author/muhammet-ali-turk/">Muhammet Ali TÜRK</a>, whose name appears under each headline. He handles reader reports and corrections. When he has read a page against its sources, the page says so with the date ("reviewed by … on …"); pages he has not yet reviewed say that too. Review dates are kept in a log and are never added without an actual review.</p>
  <h2>5. Dates on everything that changes</h2><p>Each page shows when it was last verified, and each source line shows the date it was checked. Dates are not moved without rechecking the content.</p>
  <h2>6. Nothing invented</h2><p>No invented experts, quotes, testimonials, case studies, statistics or claims of having tested products. Worked examples use hypothetical inputs and say so. Charts plot only sourced numbers on honest scales.</p>
  <h2>7. Questions come from real searches</h2><p>FAQ questions are based on Google's public search suggestions, rewritten as plain questions, so they reflect what sellers actually look for; answers come from the official sources.</p>
  <h2>8. How pages are made</h2><p>Pages are researched and drafted with the help of AI writing tools working from the primary sources, then checked as described above. The publisher is responsible for every page.</p>
  <h2>9. Photos and graphics</h2><p>Photos come from Unsplash under the Unsplash License (never Unsplash+), or from Wikimedia Commons under public-domain or Creative Commons licenses that allow commercial use without share-alike terms. Each is credited and logged with its license and source page. Photos without clear licenses, with recognizable faces, or with brand logos as the subject are not used. Photos illustrate a topic and are captioned as illustrative. Screenshots of seller dashboards are not used.</p>
  <h2>10. Funding and independence</h2><p>The site is intended to be funded by Google AdSense display advertising. Advertisers have no say in content. Ads are labeled and never placed inside tables, calculators, navigation or source lists. There are no affiliate links, sponsored posts or paid placements (<a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">disclosure</a>). Pages about seller software describe what the vendor publishes; they are not tests, ratings or endorsements, and no vendor pays for or reviews them.</p>
  <h2>11. Corrections</h2><p>Errors are corrected promptly and logged with the date and what changed on the <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>.</p>
</Page>
